<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zabulistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zabulistan was a historical region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and the upper valleys of the Helmand and Arghandab. From the late seventh century it was ruled by the Zunbil kings, who resisted the Arab conquest for nearly two centuries until the Saffarid Ya'qub ibn al-Layth took it around 870. The name survives in present-day Zabul province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zabulistan.jpg" alt="Zabulistan" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge (Great Britain) Engraver or Printer Murchison, I. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persia_with_part_of_the_Ottoman_Empire,_1872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zabulistan means the land of Zābol, a name of uncertain origin that the Afghan province of Zabul and the Iranian city of Zabol still carry; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s Shahnameh it is the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name was Jāguḍa, meaning saffron, and the Chinese monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who described it in 644, wrote it as Tsau-kü-ta. He placed it between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and the Black Range, probably the Sulaiman mountains, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and sometimes at Guzar, both strongly fortified. He recorded a cold climate with much frost and snow, abundant winter wheat, fruit and flowers, turmeric and asafoetida, and springs at Guzar used for irrigation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first mentions of the region come with its conquest by the Iranian Huns in the 4th century. Coins of the Alkhan rulers, among them Khingila I, bear the legend King of Zabol Alkhan, and after the Sasanian defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> in 484 the Nezak Shahs opened a mint at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>. The Turk Shahis took the region in the 7th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> favoured by the king, with several hundred monasteries and more than ten thousand monks, while most people worshipped the god Śuna, whose shrine on a mountain drew pilgrims and rich offerings.</p><p>Arab armies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zaranj</a> reached the region, but the historian André Wink describes the Zunbils and their kinsmen the Kabul-Shahs as rulers of a predominantly Indian rather than Persianate realm who effectively opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. C. E. Bosworth calls the Zunbils probably heirs of a southern Hephthalite or Chionite kingdom of Zabul. Under Saffarid rule, Amr ibn Layth's governor Fardaghan sacked the shrine of the god Zhun at Sakawand, and in 896 idols sent from there caused a sensation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Hindu Shahi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> later fought the Ghaznavids for the region and lost, and their victories brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of Zabulistan and the surrounding regions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zabulistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zunbil dynasty emerged in the late 7th century in the region of <b>Zabul</b>, between Ghazni and Kandahar, possibly as a branch of the Turk Shahis or of Hephthalite origin.</blockquote><a href="/state/zunbils">Zunbils</a></li></ul></section>
